"XPT Specialty Deploys Suite of AI Tools Across Wholesale Operation; Widens Market Access for Retail Agents on SME Accounts | XPT Specialty (https://xptspecialty.com/xpt-specialty-deploys-suite-of-ai-tools-across-wholesale-operation/)\nciteturn6view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn5view0\",\"lineno\":65}); Total lines: 220\nL0: cite0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com L1: \nL2: cite1†Payment Portal L3: \nL4: cite2†Client Portal L5:   * cite3†Solutions L6:     * cite4†Brokerage L7:     * cite5†Unique Products L8:     * cite6†Binding Authority L9:     * cite7†Service L10:   * cite8†Strengths L11:     * cite9†True Partners L12:     * cite10†Multiplying Value L13:   * cite11†Industries L14:     * cite12†Transportation L15:     * cite13†Hospitality L16:     * cite14†Agribusiness L17:     * cite15†Contractors L18:     * cite16†Oil & Gas L19:     * cite17†Commercial Property L20:     * cite18†Auto Dealers L21:     * cite19†Manufacturing L22:     * cite20†Farm & Ranch L23:     * cite21†Workers’ Compensation L24:     * cite22†Professional Lines L25:     * cite23†Personal Lines Products L26:     * cite24†Flood Risk Solutions L27:   * cite25†Office Locations L28:   * cite26†About Us L29:     * cite27†Management Team L30:   * cite28†News L31:     * cite28†Press Releases L32:     * cite29†Insurance Headlines L33:   * cite30†Resources L34:     * cite31†State of the Market Reports L35:     * cite32†Applications L36:     * cite33†Appointments L37:     * cite34†Broker of Records L38:   * cite30†Schedule a Call Now L39:   * cite1†Payment Portal L40:   * cite2†Client Portal L41:   * cite3†Solutions L42:     * cite4†Brokerage L43:     * cite5†Unique Products L44:     * cite6†Binding Authority L45:     * cite7†Service L46:   * cite8†Strengths L47:     * cite9†True Partners L48:     * cite10†Multiplying Value L49:   * cite11†Industries L50:     * cite12†Transportation L51:     * cite13†Hospitality L52:     * cite14†Agribusiness L53:     * cite15†Contractors L54:     * cite16†Oil & Gas L55:     * cite17†Commercial Property L56:     * cite18†Auto Dealers L57:     * cite19†Manufacturing L58:     * cite20†Farm & Ranch L59:     * cite21†Workers’ Compensation L60:     * cite22†Professional Lines L61:     * cite23†Personal Lines Products L62:     * cite24†Flood Risk Solutions L63:   * cite25†Office Locations L64:   * cite26†About Us L65:     * cite27†Management Team L66:   * cite28†News L67:     * cite28†Press Releases L68:     * cite29†Insurance Headlines L69:   * cite30†Resources L70:     * cite31†State of the Market Reports L71:     * cite32†Applications L72:     * cite33†Appointments L73:     * cite34†Broker of Records L74:   * cite30†Schedule a Call Now L75:   * cite1†Payment Portal L76:   * cite2†Client Portal L77: cite35†Contact Us L78: # XPT Specialty Deploys Suite of AI Tools Across Wholesale Operation; Widens Market Access for Retail Agents on SME Accounts\nL79: \nL80:   * October 1, 2026\nL81: \nL82: AI tools bring comparative analysis and multi-market execution into retail agents’ existing workflows, helping XPT specialists serve more clients, faster and with greater precision and insight\nL83: ## Executive Summary\nL84:   * What: Three AI tools –\nL85:     * Underwriting: comparative data analysis and quoting;\nL86:     * Binding: comparative rating with multi-market execution, and\nL87:     * Brokerage: risk analysis, market finder and multi-market execution\nL88:   * Why it matters: The tools absorb small and mid-sized enterprise (SME) processing on the wholesale side, putting up to 20 markets in front of accounts that used to receive the narrowest search.\nL89:   * Early results: in P&C, bound business jumped as much as 19.5% month-over-month, and, year-over-year, bind ratios are increasing; submissions overall are steady, while quotes are up by 6.5% year-over-year and the number of binds is up about 7.5% year-over-year since inception; in Bars and Taverns, new-business quoting is up approximately 25% year-over-year.\nL90:   * For retail agents: No new portal, no re-keying. XPT specialists review every risk and remain accountable for final placement.\nL91: \nL92: \nL93: October 1, 2026 – cite36†XPT Specialty , a leading wholesale broker for specialty commercial and personal insurance, has deployed a suite of AI tools that help retail agency partners better serve SME accounts – broadening market access and returning more bound policies on a segment historically hardest to serve at scale. XPT’s specialists guide every risk and placement decision.\nL94: “Small commercial accounts can take nearly as much work as large ones but carry a fraction of the premium, so the market has quietly underserved them for years,” said Kyle Stevens, executive partner at XPT. “These tools let us put more markets in front of more accounts and give our people more room to apply their expertise.”\nL95: “Working with XPT gives us access to a broader range of markets and more options for our clients,” said Derek S. Ross, president and COO of Kulchin Ross Insurance Services, LLC. “Their AI tools make that process even more efficient, helping identify additional opportunities and return more relevant options. It combines the market reach and expertise we value from XPT with technology that helps us deliver better solutions to our clients.”\nL96: SMEs often lack dedicated risk management resources yet face some of the most complex coverage needs. A recent cite37†JD Power study†www.jdpower.com found a 203-point satisfaction gap between SMBs whose insurer and broker truly understand their business and those who do not.\nL97: ##\nL98: ## Initial Results\nL99: XPT’s AI-powered comparative rating rolled out in the second quarter of 2026. In P&C, bound business jumped as much as 19.5% month-over-month, and, year-over-year, bind ratios are increasing. Submissions overall are steady, while quotes are up by 6.5% and the number of binds is up about 7.5% year-over-year, reflecting genuine new bind volume while maintaining underwriting quality.\nL100: In Bars and Taverns, where AI entered the workflow nine months ago, new-business quoting is up approximately 25% year-over-year. XPT expects the pattern to scale as adoption spreads to more offices and lines.\nL101: ##\nL102: ## Why Now\nL103: \nL104: SME business is often lower-margin for retail agencies, limiting how broadly those accounts get shopped. XPT’s tools absorb that processing on the wholesale side, so those accounts get shopped far more widely. SMEs gain the market breadth larger accounts typically receive and a better chance at the best overall coverage fit – not simply the lowest premium.\nL105: “Retail agents should not have to change how they work to benefit from better technology,” said Tom Ruggieri, CEO and co-founder at XPT. “We designed these tools to meet them where they are, while keeping an XPT expert accountable for the final recommendation.”\nL106: ##\nL107: ## Three Tools, One Workflow\nL108: \nL109: The suite comprises three modular tools:\nL110:   * Underwriting: Comparative Data Analysis and Comparative Quoting: Extracts and validates submission data, checks it against web and third-party sources, flags discrepancies, evaluates the risk against underwriting guidelines and recommends a price for expert review.\nL111:   * Binding: Comparative Rater with Multi-Market Execution: Sends a risk to up to 20 markets in parallel through direct system connections, with results returned to an XPT expert for final placement.\nL112:   * Brokerage: Risk Analysis, Market Finder, Multi-Market Execution: Prioritizes incoming submissions and categorizes them as simple, moderate or complex, so XPT experts focus first on the business that most needs their attention. Assesses opportunities across 100 markets and finds and prioritizes appropriate markets for quoting. Tracks communications to cull and coordinate quote information for client presentation.\nL113: ##\nL114: \nL115: ## What This Means for Retail Agents\nL116: \nL117: For retail agents, the workflow doesn’t change. XPT recommends one option alongside a summary of the alternatives, so agents can see how broadly an account was shopped. Because the model supports both brokerage and binding-authority placements, more of a client’s lines can go to a single wholesaler, and added capacity surfaces related coverage opportunities.\nL118: \nL119: ##\nL120: ## How It Was Built\nL121: \nL122: XPT co-developed the tools with external technology partners, including strategic partner cite38†Gencise AI†www.gencise.ai , around its workflows, data and expertise, rather than implementing them off-the-shelf from generic AI models; the modular design lets XPT extend to more lines over time.\nL123: \nL124: ##\nL125: ## About XPT Specialty\nL126: \nL127: XPT Specialty is a wholesale brokerage and binding firm that connects insurance professionals with specialized solutions across multiple industries. Through strong market relationships and a client-first approach, XPT helps agents find the right coverage for their insureds. For more information, visit cite36†www.xptspecialty.com .\nL128: # # # #\nL129: \nL130: Media Contact:\nL131: Caroline Traylor, 104 West for XPT Specialty\nL132: caroline.traylor@104west.com\nL133: \nL134: cite36†Home L135: \nL136: cite25†Office Locations L137: \nL138: cite26†About Us L139: \nL140: cite27†Management Team L141: \nL142: cite28†News L143: \nL144: cite3†Solutions L145: \nL146: cite4†Brokerage L147: \nL148: cite5†Unique Products L149: \nL150: cite6†Binding Authority L151: \nL152: cite7†Service L153: \nL154: cite8†Strengths L155: \nL156: cite9†True Partners L157: \nL158: cite10†Multiplying Value L159: \nL160: cite11†Industries L161: \nL162: cite12†Transportation L163: \nL164: cite13†Hospitality L165: \nL166: cite14†Agribusiness L167: \nL168: cite15†Contractors L169: \nL170: cite16†Oil & Gas L171: \nL172: cite17†Commercial Property L173: \nL174: cite18†Auto Dealers L175: \nL176: cite19†Manufacturing L177: cite20†Farm & Ranch L178: \nL179: cite21†Workers Compensation L180: \nL181: cite22†Professional Lines L182: \nL183: cite23†Products Personal L184: \nL185: cite24†Flood L186: \nL187: © 2026 cite39†XPT Group†xptholdco.com . All Rights Reserved\nL188: PRIVACY POLICY:\nL189: By texting XPT Specialty at 972-702-0500, you agree to receive Conversations (between employees) messages from XPT Specialty. Reply STOP to opt-out; Reply HELP for support; Message & data rates may apply; Messaging frequency may vary. Visit cite36†https://xptspecialty.com/ to see our privacy policy and cite36†https://xptspecialty.com/ for our Terms of Service.\nL190: \nL191: cite35†Contact Us L192: ## Contact XPT Partners\nL193: \nL194: Please enable JavaScript in your browser to complete this form.\nL195: \nL196: Name *\nL197: \nL198: [Input]First\nL199: \nL200: [Input]Last\nL201: \nL202: Phone *[Input]\nL203: \nL204: City *[Input]\nL205: \nL206: State *[Input]\nL207: \nL208: Email *[Input]\nL209: \nL210: Contact Reason *[Select]\nL211: \nL212: Message *\nL213: \nL214: Opt-in *\nL215:   * [Input]By texting XPT Specialty at 972-702-0500, you agree to receive Conversations (between employees) messages from XPT Specialty. Reply STOP to opt-out; Reply HELP for support; Message & data rates may apply; Messaging frequency may vary. Visit cite36†https://xptspecialty.com/ to see our privacy policy and cite36†https://xptspecialty.com/ for our Terms of Service.\nL216: \nL217: [Input]\nL218: \nL219: [Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Button: Submit]cite40†Image: Loading --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAutomation Today: Publication & Newsletter: Clearing the Path for Human Expertise: How Tokio Marine HCC is Automating Underwriting, Not the Underwriter (https://automationtoday.net/featuredarticles/clearing-the-path-for-human-expertise-how-tokio-marine-hcc-is-automating-underwriting-not-the-underwriter/)\nciteturn6view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn5view1\",\"lineno\":35}); Total lines: 81\nL0:   * October 6, 2026\nL1: #### NEWS FOR THE AI-POWERED INTELLIGENT AUTOMATION ECOSYSTEM\nL2: \nL3: [Input: ] [Input] [Input]\nL4: \nL5: [Input: ] [Input] [Input]\nL6: \nL7: cite0†Image: Clearing the Path for Human Expertise: How Tokio Marine HCC is Automating Underwriting, Not the Underwriter L8: Underwriting is the beating heart of the insurance business. But for a company that has evolved into one of the largest specialty insurers in the world, how it assesses risk across more than 100 products makes that process even more complex. So, while cite1†Tokio Marine HCC†www.tmhcc.com is committed to optimizing underwriting through intelligent automation, cite2†Tamer Assaad†www.linkedin.com would rather they leave the human underwriters right where they are.\nL9: Assaad, the company’s vice president of digital transformation and financial systems, oversees an intelligent automation team that grew out of a traditional RPA Center of Excellence. This evolution, he says, reflects a broader shift in what automation can achieve and how it gets there.\nL10: Agentic AI, Assaad tells cite3†Automation Today, has changed the paradigm guiding automation. Organizations should be asking themselves not if AI can automate an entire job or process, but how it can remove the barriers that keep skilled employees from applying their expertise.\nL11: The approach is now being tested in specialty insurance underwriting, where Tokio Marine HCC is preparing to put an agentic AI-based submissions-prioritization system into production. The project uses agents to examine incoming information, conduct research and assess submissions against underwriting criteria before presenting the results to human underwriters who can then focus more on the crucial work of risk assessment and less on the administrative work increasingly associated with the job.\nL12: Finding the Work Around the Work\nL13: \nL14: Specialty insurance presents an unusual automation problem. Tokio Marine HCC’s broad offering of specialty products results in many individual business lines with different risk appetites that require varied expertise.\nL15: \nL16: The underwriting project emerged after the automation team began examining submissions intake. Assaad says discussions with underwriters revealed that the most significant issue occurred before the underwriting even began.\nL17: “The submission volume you get is asymmetrical to the number of underwriters,” he explains. “There’s no way for those underwriters to look at every single submission.”\nL18: \nL19: Historically, underwriters could face submissions containing varying combinations of emails, applications, loss runs and other documents. They had to research the applicant, examine the material and compare it all against underwriting requirements before determining whether a risk was one the company wanted to consider.\nL20: Assaad says an underwriter could spend 20 or 30 minutes reviewing a submission before discovering a characteristic that should have disqualified the applicant before they even started. Meanwhile, potentially attractive opportunities might remain elsewhere in the queue, or worse, be snapped up by a competitor who addressed the customer’s need sooner.\nL21: The intelligent automation team developed a pilot system that uses multiple agents to process the incoming material. One set of agents extracts relevant information from structured and unstructured content, while others compare it with underwriting guidelines or perform outside research. The results are used to prioritize submissions according to criteria established by the insurer.\nL22: “It doesn’t do anything to replace the expertise or judgment of an underwriter. But what makes it so interesting is it clears the path,” he notes. “It really facilitates them getting to the critical submissions quickly, which has the potential to drive top-line revenue with more profitable bottom-line results.”\nL23: The system recommends which submissions deserve attention first rather than deciding whether the insurer should write the business. The underwriter, freed from more time-consuming work, can now review the prioritized submissions and use their specialized knowledge to decide whether to accept the business or not based on the merits of the case.\nL24: \nL25: Reconsidering Design\nL26: Designing a successful pilot, as many organizations have found, is relatively easy. Getting an agentic system into production takes considerably more work. To make it happen in this case, Tokio Marine HCC has had to reconsider how it designs automations.\nL27: \nL28: cite4†Image L29: Assaad says one early lesson involved the underwriting guidelines themselves. The team initially supplied the agents with an entire underwriting manual. It subsequently narrowed the material to the portions most relevant to individual assessments, which he says improved both performance and specificity.\nL30: The company also involved underwriters directly in development. Assaad says their participation served two purposes: subject-matter experts could identify process nuances the automation team might otherwise miss, and employees who would ultimately use the system had a role in shaping it from the beginning.\nL31: Tokio Marine HCC is using UiPath Agent Builder and Maestro in the project, among other technology, and brought in cite5†Ashling Partners†ashling.ai to work alongside its internal team. Assaad says the outside resources provided additional development capacity as well as another perspective when the team encountered design problems.\nL32: \nL33: Trust on The Path to Scale\nL34: As of publication, the underwriting system is in user acceptance testing and is approaching production. Assaad says the company intends to evaluate it against existing business metrics including submission-to-quote, quote-to-bind and submission-to-bind rates, as well as speed-to-quote and the amount of time underwriters spend examining submissions that ultimately are declined.\nL35: Explainability has been another consideration and vital in building trust with the underwriting team. Assaad says the system records why it assigns a particular priority or score, allowing underwriters to examine the rationale rather than simply accepting an AI-generated recommendation.\nL36: \nL37: “Underwriters were able to look at the reasoning for a decision,” he says. “Everything was auditable and explainable.”\nL38: Security and reliability are also non-negotiable in an industry as highly regulated as insurance. Assaad says Tokio Marine HCC involved its IT security organization and placed particular emphasis on prompt discipline. When information cannot be established, he would rather have the system escalate the issue for human review than manufacture an answer.\nL39: “If you can’t find it, there’s higher value in the agent saying ‘I can’t find this, elevate this particular area to human review,’ than to try to fill in the gaps,” Assaad explains.\nL40: \nL41: That philosophy also informs Assaad’s response to concerns about autonomous agents behaving unpredictably, which recently has played out in public for OpenAI and others.\nL42: “The answer to agents going rogue isn’t to avoid them altogether or avoid automation, but it’s architecting it in such a way that it can’t take any actions that you’dregret,” he says. “So, depending on what your risk appetite is, narrow that circle.”\nL43: Tokio Marine HCC will now be watching what happens as the underwriting application moves beyond testing. Assaad says the company wants to determine not only whether the system affects revenue and policy conversion, but whether prioritization changes the composition of its risk portfolio and whether faster responses affect relationships with brokers.\nL44: The longer-term opportunity he sees is applying similar approaches across additional specialty lines, despite differences in their underwriting logic. But his framing of the opportunity keeps the human role intact.\nL45: \nL46: “We didn’t automate the underwriter,” he states. “We identified human expertise as an advantage and automated everything that stood between the underwriter and them doing their best work.”\nL47: \nL48: cite6†Automation Anywhere Launches Agentic Procure-to-Pay Solution L49: cite7†Infrrd Opens Document Automation Platform to Developers With IDPForge L50: ## Recent News\nL51: \nL52:   * cite8†Report Predicts Automation Could Displace 11 Million U.S. Workers in a Decade L53:   * cite9†HSBC Deploys AI to Automate Trade Finance Document Processing L54:   * cite10†HCLSoftware Acquires Robotiq.ai L55:   * cite11†Barndoor Acquires Diaphora to Add Governed AI Workflow Automation L56:   * cite12†OpenAI Pauses Model Training Amid Concerns Over Agent Behavior L57: ### Recent News\nL58: \nL59:   * cite8†Report Predicts Automation Could Displace 11 Million U.S. Workers in a Decade L60:   * cite9†HSBC Deploys AI to Automate Trade Finance Document Processing L61:   * cite10†HCLSoftware Acquires Robotiq.ai L62:   * cite11†Barndoor Acquires Diaphora to Add Governed AI Workflow Automation L63:   * cite12†OpenAI Pauses Model Training Amid Concerns Over Agent Behavior L64: \nL65: ### Categories\nL66: \nL67:   * cite13†FeatureArticles L68:   * cite14†News L69: ### Latest News\nL70: \nL71: cite15†Image: Report Predicts Automation Could Displace 11 Million U.S. Workers in a Decade L72: \nL73: #### cite8†Report Predicts Automation Could Displace 11 Million U.S. Workers in a Decade L74: \nL75: cite16†Image: HSBC Deploys AI to Automate Trade Finance Document Processing L76: \nL77: #### cite9†HSBC Deploys AI to Automate Trade Finance Document Processing L78: \nL79: Contact us at info@AutomationToday.net (//info@automationtoday.net)\nL80: 2026 Automation Today\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLawyer for State Farm Fined $999.99 Over 7 Fake AI Case Citations | Law Commentary (https://www.lawcommentary.com/articles/state-farm-lawyer-ai-fake-cases-fine)\nciteturn6view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.lawcommentary.com/articles/state-farm-lawyer-ai-fake-cases-fine\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 105\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2: Oct 06, 2026 Newsletter\nL3: \nL4: cite1†Editions L5: \nL6: National Legal News, Information & Blogs\nL7: # Lawyer for State Farm Fined $999.99 Over 7 Fake AI Case Citations\nL8: \nL9: by cite2†Alexandra Agraz | Sep 15, 2026\nL10: \nL11: cite3†Image: State Farm sign hanging outside a brick building.†lawcommentary.sfo3.cdn.digitaloceanspaces.com Photo Source: Ken Wolter/Shutterstock.com\nL12: \nL13: A California judge has ordered a lawyer for State Farm to pay $999.99 after finding seven nonexistent case citations in pretrial filings, errors the attorney said stemmed from her use of an artificial intelligence legal research tool.\nL14: Los Angeles Superior Court Judge Elizabeth L. Bradley imposed the sanction on Jacquelene Robinson, a senior associate at Musick, Peeler & Garrett, who represents State Farm General Insurance Company in a homeowner lawsuit. The false authorities appeared in filings submitted ahead of an October trial.\nL15: Bradley’s $999.99 order falls one cent below a threshold that carries an additional consequence under California law. Courts generally must notify the State Bar when an attorney receives judicial sanctions of $1,000 or more, excluding certain discovery sanctions.\nL16: The citation problems surfaced as attorneys for homeowner Fa’alagilagi Meni-Siliga reviewed State Farm’s pretrial motions. Plaintiff attorney Eric Khodadian told the court that questionable authorities appeared in nearly every motion in limine filed on the insurer’s behalf.\nL17: Motions in limine ask a judge to decide before trial whether certain evidence or arguments can be presented to a jury. After the issue was raised during an August 7 court conference, Robinson reviewed the filings and confirmed that seven cited cases did not exist.\nL18: \nL19: The filings also included incorrect case names and quotations that could not be found in the authorities cited.\nL20: Robinson said she used Irys, an AI legal research tool, while preparing the filings and believed the service was connected to her firm’s Westlaw subscription. She also believed the program performed an internal citation check.\nL21: \nL22: She acknowledged that she relied on the tool without independently verifying every authority before the documents were filed.\nL23: In a declaration submitted before the sanctions hearing, Robinson accepted responsibility and apologized to Bradley. She told the court that citing fabricated authorities was unacceptable and said she had changed her research process.\nL24: Robinson said she now retrieves cited decisions through Westlaw, LexisNexis or another reliable source, checks quotations against the underlying opinions and conducts a citation review before filing. Authorities generated with AI must also be independently located and verified.\nL25: State Farm said it expects outside lawyers representing the company to comply with professional and ethical standards, including when using AI and reviewing court filings. The insurer said it was working with outside counsel to ensure safeguards were in place.\nL26: \nL27: The sanction comes as courts continue to confront filings containing invented cases, false quotations and other material generated by AI. Judges have increasingly imposed monetary penalties when lawyers submit unverified authorities.\nL28: Days before Bradley’s order, the New Mexico Supreme Court fined a defense attorney $5,000 after a murder appeal included fabricated witness testimony and nonexistent information that the lawyer attributed to ChatGPT.\nL29: \nL30: The State Farm filings arose from Meni-Siliga’s 2024 lawsuit over insurance coverage involving her former home in Carson, California. A 2020 fire left the property uninhabitable, and the house later suffered water damage during a storm while repairs were underway.\nL31: Meni-Siliga claims delays in handling the insurance matter contributed to the repairs remaining unfinished. State Farm disputes her allegations.\nL32: \nL33: The underlying case is scheduled for trial on October 5.\nL34: ## Share This Article\nL35: \nL36: If you found this article insightful, consider sharing it with your network.\nL37: \nL38:   *   *   *   *   * cite4†Image: Alexandra Agraz L39: \nL40: cite2†Alexandra Agraz L41: Alexandra Agraz is a former Diplomatic Aide with firsthand experience in facilitating high-level international events, including the signing of critical economic and political agreements between the United States and Mexico. She holds dual associate degrees in Humanities, Social and Political Sciences, and Film, blending a diverse academic background in diplomacy, culture, and storytelling. This unique combination enables her to provide nuanced perspectives on global relations and cultural narratives.\nL42: ## Related Articles\nL43: \nL44: cite5†Image: The U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit building in Denver, Colorado. L45: \nL46: cite6†Federal Appeals Court Moves to Crack Down on AI Filings After Lawyers Cite Fake Cases L47: A federal appeals court is moving to tighten its rules on artificial intelligence after judges encountered court filings containing fabricated cases, false quotations and other AI-g